<section class="alt">
  <div class="wrap grid two">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
      <h2>Four regimes, one spreadsheet, always out of date</h2>
      <p>
        The EU AI Act, California's automated-decision rules, China's generative AI measures and
        India's DPDP Act each have their own triggers, risk tiers and obligations, and many of them
        reach systems that are never deployed in that country. Most teams track this by hand. The
        spreadsheet goes stale the moment a model, a market or a data flow changes, and nobody can
        say why a system was rated the way it was.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">The approach</p>
      <h2>Treat compliance as software</h2>
      <ul class="points">
        <li><strong>Inventory</strong> with full version history, so every rating is tied to the metadata it was computed from.</li>
        <li><strong>Explained detection</strong>: each regime that applies comes with plain-language reasons and machine-readable signals.</li>
        <li><strong>Policy as code</strong>: Rego rules with article citations, severity and a concrete fix for every finding.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence on demand</strong>: a PDF report and a checksummed JSON manifest, backed by an append-only audit trail.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

